{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's central mechanism asserts that choosing a corroborated theory for action 'necessarily assumes' past success is a reliable guide to future success, defining this as induction. This is a false dichotomy that ignores Popper's explicit non-inductive justification: the preference is defensible because the better-corroborated theory has withstood attempts to falsify it, whereas the refuted one has not. Choosing the former is not an inductive wager that nature is uniform, but a rational decision to rely on the theory that currently best approximates truth (has highest verisimilitude) and has not yet been shown to be false. The target fails to engage with Popper's argument that acting on the best-tested theory is the only rational course *without* assuming future success, thereby mischaracterizing the 'leap' as inductive when Popper defines it as critical.","successor_question":"If the choice is based on the logical status of having survived tests rather than an expectation of future repetition, does the charge of 'smuggling induction' still hold?","target":"7d75144d16851f921bb0094b3d450c55ebdcc1d54132beb008fb275247806956"}]}